  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Monday)\nIssued at 132 PM CST TUE NOV 17 2026\n\nUpper level low across the southeast states was helping to push \nsome mid/high level clouds into the south half of Alabama this\nafternoon. Radar imagery did show a few light echoes south of\nI-85, but doubt any of that was actually reaching the ground due\nto dry low levels. That should remain the case through the \novernight hours tonight right on into the end of the week, with \nclouds and low rain chances lingering in the south and east. The \nupper low ultimately fills and gets shunted to the northeast by \nFriday, eventually taking those rain chances with it and allowing\ntemperatures continue their modest warming trend into the \nweekend. With the upper low out of the way, the upper level \npattern becomes a bit more zonal across the southern tier of the \ncountry. Surface ridging builds back in from the north, and \nconditions remain dry for central Alabama into early next week.\n\n/61/",
